                        "entry_id": "1ba09f16-cf5b-38d1-b4be-5feec4e987c0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The act of judging, judgment."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d0259fdf-9e4c-4c45-bca2-a14fd08cf152",
                                "sense_id": "c22eaac1-1043-36a9-b980-6cd86ab7fe90",
                                "text": "From this mode of bringing forward evidence, arises that last kind of dispute which we call the judication, or examination of the excuses alleged. And that is of this kind: whether it was right that his mother should be put to death by Orestes, because she had put to death Orestes's father?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f471a06b-a67a-4aff-b95c-7f453550aa54",
                                "sense_id": "c22eaac1-1043-36a9-b980-6cd86ab7fe90",
                                "text": "Moreover it is clear that in Cicero's time judication in civil as well as in criminal cases enhanced a man's dignity, which was dear to every upper-class Roman."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b76b101a-ca5c-4672-9246-1fa4aadca027",
                                "sense_id": "c22eaac1-1043-36a9-b980-6cd86ab7fe90",
                                "text": "Judication is a process in which each party offers facts and arguments to a judge or executive in order to persuade that arbiter to render an authoritative decision on its behalf."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
